00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:06.230 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello everyone my name is nari sol i'm a  pianist composer and today i want to  talk about an approach to practicing  standard repertoire or any kind of piece  of music or technique that i've been  thinking a lot about recently  [Music]  the basic concept is to compose or  improvise little excerpts that really  complement and encourage the act of  repetition which is of course very  necessary for practicing  let me explain using this example of a  passage from mephisto waltz number one  by franz lisz that i recently revisited  as you'll see it wasn't going so well  [Music]  now in order to improve how i play it i  need to do two things i need to  understand it in a way that really  serves how i play it and also put in the  reps to really get a hold of it so when  i was studying this passage i was quite  frustrated because i didn't really know  how to practice it  and i would ask my teacher  about ways to practice it and i remember  him telling me practice this until your  face turns blue  and then he gave me another tip which  was to use  [Music]  shadow  practicing which is to get  to note the destination note  [Music]  before you play it in a very fast manner  and that's very effective too but it  didn't really take me that far  and also i wasn't super motivated to  practice the section so i just remember  going back to my practice room and being  a little lost and i practiced it you  know dozens of times  and it didn't get very good  for the understanding part of it i  simplified what's going on in the right  hand to isolate the technique being used  which is a single note plus an octave of  the same pitch right above it  so my plan is to become comfortable  playing this technique  playing this kind of  pattern starting from different notes  and different transitions  so with that in mind looking at the  first measure of this section i'm  focusing on just the pitches  and with a a sharp d sharp  this pattern is applied  [Music]  now this may seem obvious but at the  time when i was practicing this passage  i didn't really think about the  formation of these notes i just focused  on  i guess practicing this until my face  turned blue  and i would just repeat it over and over  and over again  for the repetitions part i found that  simply repeating that phrase or even a  beat in its original form gets really  boring frustrating and gives me anxiety  so my solution to that is to set up a  little environment around each thing i'm  trying to work on that makes it very  inviting natural and some degree  pleasant to repeat what i'm trying to  practice  [Music]  it's sort of like  functional looping and functional  minimalism  [Laughter]  [Music]  so here are some more examples of that  [Music]  so i find this approach not only  effective but really fun to do and it's  something that i'd like to continue to  talk about and demonstrate in these  practice notes videos because i really  believe that practicing can be  extremely interesting and not  necessarily dull and boring and robotic  even for things that require a ton of  repetition  [Applause]  [Music]  so i filmed myself every time i spent  time on this in the last couple of days  to keep track of exactly how much i was  doing and i had a little less than an  hour of footage  so obviously i was revisiting this  passage so i had a bit of an advantage  but still it's worth noting how i went  from point a to point b  [Music]  so anyway i hope you found that helpful  let me know in the comments below what  are some passages that you're currently  struggling with or having difficulty  grasping  and i'll see you in the next video bye  let's find that spot  all the memories  the memories yes the memories

